  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to optimize the discrete support of the step function to minimize the max overlap. Unlike previous approaches that used SLSQP for continuous optimization, this strategy uses indicator functions (0/1) and a combinatorial minimization approach. It addresses the following bottlenecks:\n- Local optima traps in continuous space\n- Slow correlation computation\n- Insufficient exploration of binary structures\n\nThe method finds optimal discrete supports (where function is 1) by evaluating all possible subsets of size floor(n/2) at different resolutions. It uses a filtered beam search:\n\n- Starts with large lags and window-size optimizations\n- Uses analytical combinatorial optimization\n- Handles volume constraints through minimal XOR operations\n\nThe binary domain is crucial since Erd\u0151s' constant is achieved by step functions. This approach reduces correlation complexity by one order of magnitude using combinatorial structure rather than continuous relaxation.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables: n-point function h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] with mean 1\nObjective: C5 = max\u2096 \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nFor binary h (0/1), the integral becomes the overlap measure between shifted indicator sets:\n\u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx = |{x: h(x)=1, h(x+k)=0}| * dx / 2\n\nConstraints: \n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- Excatly floor(n/2) ones (since \u222bh = 1 with dx=2/n)\n\nAlgorithm structure:\n1. Multi-resolution optimization: n \u2208 {200, 250, 300}\n2. Combinatorial optimization on binary subsets\n3. Lag-mediated objective with overlap counting\n4. Batch processing with priority queue\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nA. Input handling\nRead initial_h_values(n=250) if available. Set n_list=[200,250,300] or n=best_parent_size.\n\nB. Core functions\ndef compute_score(h, n):\n    Compute all lag overlaps with analytical counts\n    dx=2/n\n    build overlap array without full correlation\n    return max_overlap\n\ndef sample_discrete(n):\n    Sample binary vector of size n with exactly floor(n/2) ones\n\ndef project_feasible(h, n):\n    Convert continuous to binary with volume adjustment\n    force even integral via minimal bit flips\n\ndef update_from_parent(h_parent, n):\n    Interpolate parent and find nearest binary subset\n\nC. Optimization phases\nPhase 1: Combinatorial binary optimization\n- Use uniform sampling with structure learning\n- Maintain beam of 100 current candidates\n- Score by max_lag_overlap\n\nPhase 2: Multi-resolution sweep\nFor n in n_list (descending):\n    Find local optimum\n    Update best solution\n    Use parent interpolation if n=250\n\nD. Convergence checks\n- Early kill if top score below 0.3808\n- Minimal improvement threshold\n- Budget-aware termination\n\n4. Search choices\nResolution: Start from coarsest n=300 for n, then 250, 200\nBeam size: 100 candidates with top autoregressive path\nWindow-size: 20-30 points for combinatorial neighborhood\nParent exploitation: Use interpolation and subset expansion\n\n5. Compute budget\nTotal phases: 3 (binary, combinatorial, refinement)\nEstimated time:\n```\nPhase 1: 500s (n=300)\nPhase 2: 400s (n=250)\nPhase 3: 200s (n=200)\n```\nDeadline management:\n- Phase completion checks\n- Early termination if time critical\n- Sample reduction for tight deadlines\n\n6. Validation and return\nCheck:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h[i] = n/2 exactly\n- Correlation objective \u2265 -5 log(n)\n\nReturn(h_best, c5_score, n)",
